# Line-ending policy.
#
# Without this file, a Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true` (the default from Git
# for Windows' installer) rewrites every text file in the working tree to CRLF. Git's
# stored blobs stay LF, so the repository is fine and a Linux or CI checkout is fine — but
# `docker build` copies the *working tree*, and a shell script with a CRLF shebang makes
# the kernel look for an interpreter called `/bin/sh\r`.
#
# The symptom is this, with nothing else to go on:
#
#     exec ./docker-entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory
#
# which reads as a missing file rather than a line-ending problem and sends you looking in
# entirely the wrong place. The image builds successfully and then refuses to start.
#
# So: anything executed by a Unix kernel or interpreted by a shell is pinned to LF,
# regardless of the platform it was checked out on.

* text=auto

# Shell scripts and container entrypoints — these are the ones that actually break.
*.sh text eol=lf
docker-entrypoint.sh text eol=lf

# Dockerfiles and compose files: tolerate CRLF today, but there is no reason to risk it.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
Dockerfile.* text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf
docker-compose*.yml text eol=lf

# Prisma schema and migrations are read by a Linux-side engine.
*.prisma text eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf binary
*.traineddata binary
